### What is heap sort?

Heap sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm built on a binary max-heap stored directly inside the array. A max-heap ensures that every parent is at least as large as its children, which forces the overall maximum to sit at index zero. The algorithm has two phases: build the heap, then repeatedly extract the maximum.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class HeapSort {

    public static void heapSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;

        // Phase 1: Build max-heap
        for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            siftDown(arr, n, i);
        }

        // Phase 2: Sort down
        for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; i--) {
            swap(arr, 0, i);       // move max to end
            siftDown(arr, i, 0);   // restore heap over shrinking range
        }
    }

    private static void siftDown(int[] arr, int heapSize, int parent) {
        int largest = parent;
        int left = 2 * parent + 1;
        int right = 2 * parent + 2;

        if (left < heapSize && arr[left] > arr[largest]) largest = left;
        if (right < heapSize && arr[right] > arr[largest]) largest = right;

        if (largest != parent) {
            swap(arr, parent, largest);
            siftDown(arr, heapSize, largest);
        }
    }

    private static void swap(int[] a, int i, int j) {
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {50000, 20000, 80000, 10000, 40000, 70000};
        heapSort(amounts);
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + Arrays.toString(amounts));
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted: [10000, 20000, 40000, 50000, 70000, 80000]
```

### How does the array represent a binary heap?

For an element at index i, its left child is at `2*i + 1`, its right child is at `2*i + 2`, and its parent is at `(i - 1) / 2`. No pointers are needed. The heap is a complete binary tree packed into a contiguous array.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class HeapIndices {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] heap = {80, 50, 70, 20, 40, 10};
        System.out.println("Heap array: " + java.util.Arrays.toString(heap));
        for (int i = 0; i < heap.length; i++) {
            int left = 2 * i + 1;
            int right = 2 * i + 2;
            System.out.println("  index " + i + " (" + heap[i] + ")"
                + (left < heap.length ? " left=" + heap[left] : "")
                + (right < heap.length ? " right=" + heap[right] : ""));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Heap array: [80, 50, 70, 20, 40, 10]
  index 0 (80) left=50 right=70
  index 1 (50) left=20 right=40
  index 2 (70) left=10
  index 3 (20)
  index 4 (40)
  index 5 (10)
```

### How does sift-down work?

Sift-down takes a node that may violate the heap property, compares it to its larger child, and swaps down repeatedly until the node is at least as large as both children or reaches a leaf.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class SiftDownTrace {

    static void siftDown(int[] arr, int size, int parent) {
        int largest = parent;
        int left = 2 * parent + 1;
        int right = 2 * parent + 2;
        if (left < size && arr[left] > arr[largest]) largest = left;
        if (right < size && arr[right] > arr[largest]) largest = right;
        if (largest != parent) {
            System.out.println("  Swap " + arr[parent] + " with " + arr[largest]);
            int t = arr[parent]; arr[parent] = arr[largest]; arr[largest] = t;
            siftDown(arr, size, largest);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 80, 70, 20, 40, 50}; // 10 at root violates
        System.out.println("Before: " + java.util.Arrays.toString(arr));
        siftDown(arr, arr.length, 0);
        System.out.println("After:  " + java.util.Arrays.toString(arr));
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [10, 80, 70, 20, 40, 50]
  Swap 10 with 80
  Swap 10 with 40
After:  [80, 40, 70, 20, 10, 50]
```

### Why is build-heap O(n) and not O(n log n)?

Building the heap by sifting down from the last parent toward the root is O(n), not O(n log n). The reason is that most nodes are near the bottom where the sift distance is small. Half the nodes are leaves (zero work), a quarter sift one level, an eighth sift two levels, and so on. The total work sums to O(n).

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class BuildHeapComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // For n elements:
        // n/2 leaves: 0 sifts each
        // n/4 nodes: 1 sift each
        // n/8 nodes: 2 sifts each
        // Sum: n/4*1 + n/8*2 + n/16*3 + ... = O(n)
        System.out.println("Build-heap is O(n), not O(n log n)");
        System.out.println("Most nodes are near the bottom with short sift distances.");
    }
}
```

```text
Build-heap is O(n), not O(n log n)
Most nodes are near the bottom with short sift distances.
```

### How does heap sort compare to quick sort and merge sort?

| Feature | Heap Sort | Quick Sort | Merge Sort |
|---|---|---|---|
| Time (worst) | O(n log n) | O(n^2) | O(n log n) |
| Time (avg) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) |
| Space | O(1) | O(log n) | O(n) |
| Stable | No | No | Yes |
| In-place | Yes | Yes | No |
| Cache | Poor (jumping) | Good (sequential) | Sequential reads |

### Why is heap sort not the default despite its guarantees?

Heap sort guarantees O(n log n) in all cases with O(1) space, but its memory-access pattern jumps around the array following parent-child indices. This is cache-unfriendly and makes it slower in practice than quick sort's sequential scans. Quick sort wins for speed; merge sort wins for stability. Heap sort's niche is when you need in-place plus guaranteed O(n log n).

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class HeapVsQuickBenchmark {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 1_000_000;
        int[] data = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) data[i] = (int)(Math.random() * 1_000_000);

        // Quick sort (via Arrays.sort)
        int[] copy1 = data.clone();
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        Arrays.sort(copy1);
        System.out.println("Arrays.sort: " + (System.nanoTime()-start)/1_000_000 + "ms");

        // Heap sort
        int[] copy2 = data.clone();
        start = System.nanoTime();
        heapSort(copy2);
        System.out.println("Heap sort:   " + (System.nanoTime()-start)/1_000_000 + "ms");
    }

    static void heapSort(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = n/2-1; i >= 0; i--) siftDown(arr, n, i);
        for (int i = n-1; i > 0; i--) {
            int t=arr[0]; arr[0]=arr[i]; arr[i]=t;
            siftDown(arr, i, 0);
        }
    }

    static void siftDown(int[] a, int sz, int p) {
        int lg=p, l=2*p+1, r=2*p+2;
        if(l<sz&&a[l]>a[lg])lg=l; if(r<sz&&a[r]>a[lg])lg=r;
        if(lg!=p){int t=a[p];a[p]=a[lg];a[lg]=t;siftDown(a,sz,lg);}
    }
}
```

```text
Arrays.sort: 80ms
Heap sort:   200ms
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Phase | Time | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Build heap | O(n) | Most nodes near bottom |
| Sort down | O(n log n) | n extractions at log n each |
| Total | O(n log n) | All cases |
| Space | O(1) | In-place |

### How is the heap structure used beyond sorting?

The heap is far more commonly used as a priority queue than for sorting. Java's `PriorityQueue` is a min-heap that supports O(log n) insert and extract-min. It is used for Dijkstra's algorithm, merge-K-sorted-lists, and scheduling tasks by priority.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class PriorityQueueUsage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
        pq.offer(50000);
        pq.offer(10000);
        pq.offer(30000);

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Poll: " + pq.poll());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Poll: 10000
Poll: 30000
Poll: 50000
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is an off-by-one error in the child index bounds check, allowing an access beyond the active heap size. Another mistake is forgetting that sift-down must be recursive (or iterative) until the node reaches its final position.
